# The player: it keeps the two feeds moving. Program is what goes out; Preview is what is next.
#
# WHY THIS IS A SERVER TASK AND NOT A GUI SUB-TASK. A gui sub-task is tagged end_on_new_gui, so
# it dies the moment the operator repaints the page or steps onto another tab. Playback must
# outlive all of that - the whole point is that the show keeps running while the director goes
# off to build the next item. So it is scheduled ONCE from the top level, idles cheaply when
# nothing is playing, and reads its instructions from the shared cv_* variables.
#
# Scheduled once, at story load, rather than on every Send: scheduling on Send would stack a
# second player on the second press, and two players advancing the same rundown at different
# offsets is a feed that flickers.
#
# IT TICKS FASTER THAN THE DWELL. A half-second tick with a counter, not a sleep of cv_dwell:
# preview has to follow the editor as it is typed in, and a camera leg re-issues when the leg
# ENDS (a 22-second dolly) rather than when the rundown advances.
task_schedule(cv_play_task)

default shared cv_air = ""

=== cv_play_task
    cv_play_elapsed = 0.0
--- cv_play_loop
    await delay_app(0.5)

    # Every declared screen, re-read each tick: consoles connect, disconnect and change mode
    # behind this, and a screen's MODE is its selection - there is no picker any more.
    cv_prog = director_program_screens()
    cv_prev = director_preview_screens()
    director_play_forget_absent(cv_prog + cv_prev)

    # RE-EVALUATED EVERY TICK, not captured at Send. A generator rundown tracks ships that
    # spawn and die, and the play set drops items whose subject has gone - so a rundown does
    # not slowly rot into a list of dead contacts over a long session.
    cv_play_items = []
    if cv_playing:
        cv_play_items = director_rundown_play_set(cv_rundowns)

    # THE DWELL IS COUNTED, NOT SLEPT, and the decision belongs to director_play_feeds - whether
    # to advance depends on the HELD item's own hold, so deciding out here would mean knowing
    # what is held before asking what is held. Hand the clock over and take back what it says.
    #
    # It is also the only thing that may move the feed. The play set is rebuilt every tick so
    # generated rundowns track ships that spawn and die, and "The action" re-sorts it by live
    # excitement each time - so anything positional would drift twelve times a dwell.
    cv_play_elapsed = cv_play_elapsed + 0.5

    # TWO FEEDS, ONE ITEM EACH, ONE EVALUATION. Every program screen shows the same thing and
    # every preview screen shows the same thing - that is broadcast, and it is what replaced a
    # per-screen distribution nobody wanted. One call, so preview's "next up" is computed
    # against the same ordering program just used.
    cv_feeds = director_play_feeds(cv_play_items, cv_play_elapsed, cv_dwell, cv_auto, cv_punch)
    cv_prog_item = cv_feeds["program"]
    cv_prev_item = cv_feeds["preview"]
    cv_play_elapsed = cv_feeds["elapsed"]
    cv_play_plan = director_play_plan(cv_prog_item, cv_prog) + director_play_plan(cv_prev_item, cv_prev)

    for cv_p in cv_play_plan:
        # `changed` is what keeps this cheap: a reroute is a full page rebuild on the target
        # screen and restarts its camera, and re-issuing one that is already correct twice a
        # second would make both feeds unwatchable. A furniture-only change never reaches here
        # at all - director_play_plan re-shows the cards in place.
        if cv_p["changed"]:
            cv_p_item = cv_p["item"]
            if cv_p_item is None:
                director_play_release(cv_p["screen"])
                gui_reroute_client(cv_p["screen"], cv_screen_home)
            elif cv_p_item["kind"] == "con":
                gui_reroute_client(cv_p["screen"], cv_show, {"cv_ship_id": cv_p_item["ship"], "cv_console": cv_p_item["console"], "cv_shot": None})
            elif cv_p_item["mode"] == "tactical":
                # A 2D view, not a camera - the screen keeps its own cam and the view is
                # centered per CLIENT, so two screens can hold different modes for one ship.
                gui_reroute_client(cv_p["screen"], cv_show_2d, {"cv_subject": cv_p_item["subject"]})
            else:
                # A camera item needs a screen that can SHOW a camera, so the screen is put on
                # the cinematic console (a full-screen 3dview) and cv_show applies the shot
                # once that console exists. Applying the camera first would aim a view the
                # screen does not have yet.
                gui_reroute_client(cv_p["screen"], cv_show, {"cv_ship_id": cv_p_item["subject"], "cv_console": "cinematic", "cv_shot": cv_p_item})

    # LEGS ADVANCE EVERY TICK, independent of `changed`: a 22-second dolly on a screen that is
    # not changing item would otherwise run once and freeze on its last frame. A chase re-aims
    # here too, which is what keeps it behind a turning ship.
    director_play_tick_shots()

    # A SEPARATE LINE FROM cv_status. The operator's own messages ("added 3 console items")
    # would be wiped half a second after they appeared if the player wrote over them.
    shared cv_air = director_play_status(cv_prog_item, len(cv_prog), len(cv_prev), cv_auto, cv_punch is not None)
    jump cv_play_loop


# Back to whichever holding page this screen belongs on. ONE label, so the player has one None
# branch instead of two nearly-identical ones - the mode already knows which page it is.
=== cv_screen_home
    if director_mode_of(client_id) == "preview":
        jump cv_screen_preview
    jump cv_screen_program
